ith", "No general-election polling exists, so the forecast rests on fundamentals, ratings, and markets", "Polymarket prices Smith's win chances at only about 7 percent"], "rationale": "The district's Republican lean, Allen's incumbency and overwhelming fundraising advantage, and a unanimous Safe/Solid R consensus among raters all point the same direction, and prediction markets agree. Smith's campaign has shown minimal financial capacity, leaving little evidence to move the forecast toward competitiveness.", "watch": "Any credible polling showing Allen under 55 percent, or a surge of outside spending for Smith, would be the first sign the race is tightening.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Rick Allen (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Rick Allen, the Republican incumbent, seeks another term in a district that leaned Republican by about seven points at the last presidential election. He enters the general election with roughly $1.3 million in cash on hand after raising nearly $1 million, while Democratic nominee Ceretta Smith, who won her primary runoff in June, has raised under $27,000 and holds only a few thousand dollars available.\n\nEvery major race rater places this seat in the Safe or Solid Republican column, and prediction markets give Smith roughly a 7 percent chance of an upset. No general-election polling is available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals, ratings, and money. Allen's constituent-focused headlines and Smith's endorsement from Common Defense have not altered the race's basic shape, which remains firmly Republican-favored.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0633, "margin": -10.05}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0456, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0466, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0433, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0323, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27"